{
  "answer_text": "Reusing a request ID within the same session violates the protocol requirement that every request include a non-null, previously-unused ID, breaking the protocol’s per-session ID uniqueness assumption. Implementations therefore must ensure each request includes a non-null string or integer ID that has not been previously used by that requestor in the same session.",
  "source_quote": "Requests **MUST** include a string or integer ID.\n* Unlike base JSON-RPC, the ID **MUST NOT** be `null`.\n* The request ID **MUST NOT** have been previously used by the requestor within the same\n  session."
}

{
  "answer_text": "The provided MCP Registry preview does not describe security or trust‑boundary risks related to treating arbitrary _meta metadata as authoritative, nor does it specify how implementations should constrain or validate _meta. Instead, the blog points readers to the project’s OpenAPI specification and moderation guidelines as the authoritative sources for schemas and policy details—those documents should be consulted for any metadata security and implementation guidance.",
  "source_quote": "As part of the MCP project, the MCP Registry, as well as a parent [OpenAPI specification](https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/registry/blob/main/docs/reference/api/official-registry-api.md), are open source—allowing everyone to build a compatible sub-registry."
}

{
  "answer_text": "MCP treats Notifications as one-way messages: they MUST NOT include an ID and the receiver MUST NOT send any response. If either rule is broken (a Notification includes an ID or a receiver replies), that behavior violates the MCP/JSON-RPC notification contract and is not permitted by the protocol—implementations should treat such messages as protocol violations (e.g., reject, ignore, or otherwise consider them invalid).",
  "source_quote": "Notifications are sent from the client to the server or vice versa, as a one-way message.\nThe receiver **MUST NOT** send a response.\n...\n* Notifications **MUST NOT** include an ID."
}

{
  "answer_text": "If an implementation uses STDIO as its transport, it SHOULD NOT follow the HTTP-based authorization guidance in this specification and should instead retrieve credentials from the environment. Because STDIO and other transports may not provide an authorization context, credential handling becomes a host-local trust boundary: receivers should document this limitation, bind tasks to an auth context when available, and otherwise generate cryptographically secure task IDs and consider shorter TTLs to reduce exposure. In short, credentials are managed locally and implementers must protect local credential storage/access and clearly document any custom authentication limitations.",
  "source_quote": "Implementations using an HTTP-based transport **SHOULD** conform to this specification,\nwhereas implementations using STDIO transport **SHOULD NOT** follow this specification,\nand instead retrieve credentials from the environment."
}

{
  "answer_text": "The SDK page itself does not enumerate concrete security, consent, or token controls; it focuses on SDK capabilities and links to the authorization/security guidance. For concrete requirements (scoped delegation, consent flows, token lifetimes/revocation, audit/logging, least-privilege policies, etc.) you must follow the platform\u0002s authorization and security documentation—see the \"Understanding Authorization in MCP\" tutorial linked from the SDK page.",
  "source_quote": "Understanding Authorization in MCP"
}

{
  "answer_text": "When MCP UI components use the MCP JSON-RPC base protocol (over postMessage), UI authors must implement and consume standard JSON-RPC 2.0 messages: build well-formed requests/notifications, handle responses and JSON-RPC error objects, and use the protocol methods shown in the spec (e.g., resources/list, resources/read). Hosts must implement server-side validation and enforcement: validate resource URIs, enforce access controls and permissions, ensure binary data is properly encoded, and return standard JSON-RPC errors for failure cases. Runtime failure modes to handle include resource-not-found and internal errors (mapped to the documented JSON-RPC error codes), malformed or mis-encoded resource data, and access/permission failures — all of which should be surfaced as JSON-RPC errors per the spec.",
  "source_quote": "Servers **SHOULD** return standard JSON-RPC errors for common failure cases:

* Resource not found: `-32002`
* Internal errors: `-32603`

Example error:

```
{
  "jsonrpc": "2.0",
  "id": 5,
  "error": {
    "code": -32002,
    "message": "Resource not found",
    "data": {
      "uri": "file:///nonexistent.txt"
    }
  }
}
```

... 

## [\u200b](#security-considerations) Security Considerations

1. Servers **MUST** validate all resource URIs
2. Access controls **SHOULD** be implemented for sensitive resources
3. Binary data **MUST** be properly encoded
4. Resource permissions **SHOULD** be checked before operations"
}

{
  "answer_text": "Pre-declaring UI templates as ui:// URIs and separating static presentation from dynamic tool data lets hosts fetch, review/validate, and cache templates ahead of execution, improving security, consistency and runtime performance while keeping tool outputs as pure data to populate an already-declared UI. Servers are responsible for registering UI resources (providing a uri, name and mimeType) and referencing those URIs from tool metadata. Hosts are responsible for fetching, validating and (optionally) caching those templates before rendering, treating tool results only as data for the template, and falling back to a text-only response if a template is missing or untrusted.",
  "source_quote": "// Server registers UI resource
{
  uri: "ui://charts/bar-chart",
  name: "Bar Chart Viewer",
  mimeType: "text/html+mcp"
}

// Tool references it in metadata
{
  name: "visualize_data_as_bar_chart",
  description: "Plots some data as a bar chart",
  inputSchema: {
    type: "object",
    properties: {
      series: { type: "array", items: .... }
    }
  },
  _meta: {
    "ui/resourceUri": "ui://charts/bar-chart",
  }
}"
}

{
  "answer_text": "Servers must provide meaningful text-only or structured fallbacks for all UI-enabled tools and must not assume the ui:// UI will be rendered; they should return useful non-UI content so the tool remains usable. Hosts must detect when they cannot or will not render a ui:// template (for example due to security policy, lack of support, or user denial) and switch to the server’s text-based flow rather than attempting partial or undefined UI execution. This mutual graceful degradation avoids broken workflows and undefined behavior.",
  "source_quote": "MCP Apps is an optional extension. Existing implementations continue working without changes, and hosts can gradually adopt UI support at their own pace. Servers should provide text-only fallback for all UI-enabled tools and return meaningful content even when UI is unavailable, so they can serve both UI-capable and text-only hosts."
}

{
  "answer_text": "Using registry-listed public MCP servers gives easy discovery and faster integration by relying on a central catalog and removes the need to host and operate the server yourself, making it suitable for experimentation or low-risk workloads. The tradeoff is reduced control over availability, security, privacy, versioning, and SLAs; hosting a custom/self-hosted MCP server restores that control but shifts responsibilities to your team for designing, operating, securing, and upgrading the service. For production workloads prefer self-hosting or strictly vetted/allowlisted registry entries with clear SLAs and governance to ensure availability, compliance, and tenant isolation.",
  "source_quote": "Central catalog of publicly available MCP servers for discovery and implementation"
}

{
  "answer_text": "I can’t extract the required vetting, runtime safeguards, or data-flow controls because the provided source material is missing — the Source Material field contains only a placeholder. Please supply the actual page_content so I can read it and give a grounded, source-backed answer.",
  "source_quote": "This would be in the page_content field"
}

{
  "answer_text": "If a client or registry UI assumes the server always returns the latest-compatible versions or if it caches server metadata that later changes, clients can experience runtime incompatibilities (broken schemas, method-not-found or deserialization errors), unexpected capability or auth changes, and loss of reproducibility when pinned versions silently change. To remain robust, clients should explicitly request and validate version identifiers (and support pinning), expose and allow selection of specific versions instead of only showing latest, perform strict schema/capability negotiation and feature-gating, use cached manifests with TTLs or revalidation tied to version IDs, and treat custom endpoints as independent authorities requiring full version and capability checks.",
  "source_quote": "Show only latest versions"
}

{
  "answer_text": "If the Official MCP Registry or its UI shows “Loading servers...” or a “Retry” option, clients should assume discovery may be transiently slow or unavailable (network/DNS/TLS problems, rate-limiting, or a full registry outage) and plan for failed or delayed responses. Resilient strategies include retries with exponential backoff and jitter (with a capped retry budget), circuit-breaker / fail-fast behavior to avoid overloading the registry, local caching of validated registry entries/manifests with TTLs and integrity checks, and operator-configurable fallback or pinned endpoints for critical flows; surface diagnostics/metrics and require operator approval for sensitive operations when discovery is degraded. These measures let clients continue in degraded/read-only modes where safe and avoid accidental use of unvetted servers during registry outages.",
  "source_quote": "Loading servers...\n\nRetry"
}

{
  "answer_text": "Reusing a request ID within the same MCP session violates the protocol requirement that an ID “MUST NOT have been previously used by the requestor within the same session,” so such reuse breaks the protocol’s ID-uniqueness requirement and renders the session non-compliant. MCP implementations must therefore generate request IDs as a string or integer (not null), validate that incoming IDs are of those types and have not been used before by that requestor in the session, and enforce the prohibition on reuse for the lifetime of the session.",
  "source_quote": "Requests MUST include a string or integer ID.\n* Unlike base JSON-RPC, the ID MUST NOT be `null`.\n* The request ID MUST NOT have been previously used by the requestor within the same\n  session."
}

{
  "answer_text": "MCP implementations MAY support sending JSON-RPC batches, but they MUST support receiving JSON-RPC batches. JSON-RPC batches are sent as an array containing multiple requests and/or notifications, so implementations (especially servers) must be prepared to accept and process array-encoded batches even if they themselves do not send batches.",
  "source_quote": "JSON-RPC also defines a means to\n[batch multiple requests and notifications](https://www.jsonrpc.org/specification#batch),\nby sending them in an array. MCP implementations MAY support sending JSON-RPC\nbatches, but MUST support receiving JSON-RPC batches."
}

